<ul>
<li><b>Plunge to sauna in one step:</b> the cold plunge moves about 4 ft toward the doors, and the sauna sits right behind it against the stair wall with its 69″ door side facing the tub. Between them is a 30″ wet mat; the door needs 26½″ to swing open, so it clears the tub. The plunge’s chiller sits at the tub’s stair end, out of the walkway.</li>
<li><b>Walkways:</b> 36″ from the back of the lift to the plunge (the walk from the house door), 41–65″ between the platform and the sauna and plunge, and about 52″ across the back of the room to the back door. Parking, the lift and the platform don’t move.</li>
<li><b>Gear rearranged:</b> the reverse hyper goes behind the sauna against the stair wall; the GHD moves to the back wall beside the back-door landing; the Nordic hamstring bench stays under the loft with a clear fall lane; the flat and hip-thrust benches park along the sauna’s side and pull out onto the platform. All of it is easy to move later.</li>
<li><b>Power (planned with the installer):</b> (A) the sauna’s own 240V / 20A circuit with a NEMA 6-20 outlet behind it; (B) a dedicated 120V GFCI circuit for the plunge’s chiller; (C) a spare 120V GFCI outlet for the red light panel on its own, a fan or a speaker. All three are on the stair wall in one conduit run, so no cords cross the floor. GFCI-protected; not on the backup batteries.</li>
<li><b>Features:</b> Canadian hemlock cabin, app preheating and scheduling, chromotherapy lighting ring, built-in high-heat Bluetooth speakers, tilted backrest, near-zero EMF rating, about a 1-hour assembly. Indoor use only.</li>
<li><b>Red light therapy panel:</b> mounts in the sauna door and lifts out for use elsewhere (door hook included; a floor stand is optional). Seven wavelengths of blue, red and near-infrared light: 480, 630, 660, 810, 830, 850 and 1060 nm. It is built for sauna heat and comes with its own standard plug-in cord for use outside the sauna.</li>
<li><b>Why the garage:</b> about 11½–12½ ft ceiling, a concrete slab floor, a heated room, and the cold plunge one step away. Cautions: no engines running while it’s in use, keep the maker’s clearances, set it on the bare slab or a cut-out in the mats, and check where the floor drain is before final placement.</li>
</ul>
